[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
OpenSearch 2.19.7 was installed (of azsupport/ports).

Please see %%ETCDIR%% for a sample version of
opensearch.yml.

opensearch-security-analytics reads its prepackaged rules from the
directory of the configuration: %%ETCDIR%%/opensearch-security-analytics
(files of this package). A profile with a configuration of its own
(opensearch_<profile>_config) needs that directory there too:

cp -Rp %%ETCDIR%%/opensearch-security-analytics /path/of/the/profile/

This package has no security plugin (no TLS, no users and passwords): a
setting of it in opensearch.yml stops the start. The rc script refuses
to start with one and names its lines; remove them, such as

plugins.security.disabled: true

and every other plugins.security.* (and opendistro_security.*) line.
Without the plugin, everyone who reaches the HTTP and transport ports
can read and change every index: keep them behind the firewall.

Not in this package: opensearch-security, opensearch-knn (its engines are
native code of Linux), opensearch-neural-search (it needs opensearch-knn)
and opensearch-performance-analyzer (Linux only); every other plugin of
the bundle is. An index of opensearch-knn (index.knn, a knn_vector field)
does not open on this node, an ingest or search pipeline with a processor
of opensearch-neural-search does not run on it, and a cluster whose state
holds opensearch-knn-blocked-models (after a model of k-NN was deleted)
does not take it as a node. opensearch-ml runs its remote models
(connectors) only: its local models need native code of Linux, and the
two jars of that code (ONNX Runtime with CUDA, the tokenizers of DJL:
307 MiB) are not in this package.

With bootstrap.memory_lock: true in opensearch.yml OpenSearch locks its
memory (the whole heap, at the start). The rc script runs it in the
login class root, whose memorylocked is unlimited; the two sysctls it
needs are the defaults of FreeBSD:

sysctl security.bsd.unprivileged_mlock=1
sysctl vm.max_user_wired

(pages of 4 KB; above 8400000 for a heap of 31g).

The heap of this server is set in /etc/rc.conf, as -Xmx takes it (-Xms and
-Xmx get the same size). Without it the heap is the 31g of jvm.options,
which is for a server with 64 GB of RAM and more: with less RAM set it, at
most half of the RAM (the rest is the cache of the file system):

opensearch_heap="4g"

OpenSearch of this package keeps no archives of old logs: path.logs holds
the files of each log, in JSON and in text (<cluster>_server.json and
<cluster>.log, the deprecation, slow and task logs), and a file that
reaches its size (128 MB the server logs, 1 GB the others) starts anew,
the old one is deleted (%%ETCDIR%%/log4j2.properties). It writes no GC
log and no heap dump. The archives that are there already
(<cluster>-<date>-<n>.json.gz and .log.gz, the others .gz, gc.log*) stay
until removed by hand.

From opensearch217: pkg install opensearch219 takes its place, on the
same /var/db/opensearch. Upgrade the data nodes first and the nodes
eligible as cluster manager last (a node of 2.19 joins a cluster
manager of 2.17, not the other way). The exporters of query-insights
per metric of 2.17 (search.insights.top_queries.cpu.exporter.* and
search.insights.top_queries.latency.exporter.*) are not in 2.19, which
has one exporter of all (search.insights.top_queries.exporter.*): such
persistent settings become archived.* when the cluster manager of 2.19
starts, and a new PUT _cluster/settings with them is refused as not
recognized. Once every node runs 2.19, remove the archived ones (the
http.port of the node in place of 9200):

curl -s -X PUT -H 'Content-Type: application/json' http://127.0.0.1:9200/_cluster/settings -d '{"persistent":{"archived.*":null}}'

Its temporary directory is /tmp/opensearch, made anew at each start
(opensearch_tmpdir in /etc/rc.conf for another place).

When running within a jail, it's highly advisable to set:

enforce_statfs = 1

for the jail running opensearch instance.
EOM
}
]
